        (5, 6, """Creates the two long-lived objects. `@StateObject` means "this view owns this object, create it once and keep it alive for as long as the app runs, and redraw the screens when it announces a change". `private` means only this struct can touch them directly."""),
        (7, 7, """Reads the app's *scene phase*: whether the app is active (on screen), inactive (switching apps or showing the app switcher) or in the background. `\\.scenePhase` is a key path into the environment; `@Environment` keeps the property up to date."""),
        (9, 10, """`body` is where an `App` describes its content: here, one `WindowGroup`, which is the window the app shows. `some Scene` is Swift's way of saying "a scene whose exact type you do not need to spell out"."""),
        (11, 13, """The window's content is a `ContentView`. `.environmentObject(...)` hands the two objects to `ContentView` and everything inside it, so any screen can declare `@EnvironmentObject var bluetooth: BluetoothManager` and receive the same shared instance instead of having to pass it by hand through every level."""),
        (14, 14, """When the window first appears, connect the two objects: the Bluetooth manager gets a reference to the history store so it can save each new packet. This cannot be done in the property declarations because each `@StateObject` is created independently."""),
