PROTO = dict(
    path="ios/HealthWatch/BLEProtocol.swift",
    lang="swift",
    title="BLEProtocol.swift: understanding the 20-byte packet",
    intro="""This file mirrors the firmware's `Bluetooth.cpp` on the phone side. The packet layout is fixed by agreement between the two: change one side and the other must change with it.""",
    items=[
        ("F", "ble_packet", "The packet again, as seen by `decode` below."),
        (1, 1, """`Foundation` is Apple's base library: `UUID`, `Data`, `Date`."""),
        (3, 6, """`WatchProtocol` is an `enum` with no cases, used purely as a named place to hold two constants. They are the same 128-bit identifiers as in `Bluetooth.cpp`, written as text and converted to a `UUID`. The `!` at the end means "I promise this text is a valid UUID": if it were not, the app would crash at start-up, which is the right behaviour for a typo in a constant."""),
        (8, 9, """The activity types the watch can report. `: UInt8` means each case is stored as a one-byte number; `: Codable` means the type can be converted to and from JSON for saving. `case unknown = 0, stationary, walking, ...` numbers the cases 0, 1, 2, 3, 4 (each one more than the last), which matches the firmware: 1 is stationary, 2 is walking. The simplified firmware only ever sends 1 or 2."""),
        (10, 19, """`title` is a *computed property*: a value worked out on demand. A `switch` picks the display text for each case; Swift insists every case is handled."""),
        (21, 22, """The signal quality levels, numbered 0 to 3 to match the firmware's byte 3."""),
        (23, 31, """The display text for each quality level."""),
        (33, 33, """`WatchSnapshot` is one reading from the watch. A `struct` is a bundle of values. `Codable` lets it be saved as JSON; `Identifiable` means each snapshot can be told apart from the others, which lists need."""),
        (34, 34, """`id` is built from three fields so two snapshots never share it: the device, the watch's uptime and the packet sequence number. `\\(...)` inside a string inserts a value."""),
        (35, 36, """`let` makes a value fixed once set. `deviceID` is the phone's identifier for this watch; `receivedAt` is the phone's clock when the packet arrived."""),
        (37, 40, """Four numbers straight from the packet: the sequence number, the watch's uptime in milliseconds, the step total and the cadence. `UInt32` and `UInt16` are unsigned 32 and 16 bit integers."""),
        (41, 42, """Heart rate and oxygen are *optionals*, marked `?`. An optional is either a value or `nil` (nothing). Heart rate is `nil` when the watch says it is not valid, which is why the screen can honestly say "Unavailable" instead of showing a stale or zero number. Oxygen is always `nil` because the firmware does not measure it."""),
        (43, 46, """The four flag bits turned into true/false values: whether each sensor works, whether the pulse sensor has contact, and a possible-fall flag (never set by the simplified firmware)."""),
        (47, 48, """The quality and activity as the enum types above."""),
        (50, 50, """`decode` turns the 20 raw bytes into a `WatchSnapshot`. `static` means it is called on the type (`WatchSnapshot.decode(...)`), not on an existing snapshot. `-> Self?` says it returns a snapshot or `nil`. The `date` parameter defaults to the current time."""),
        (51, 51, """Copies the incoming `Data` into an array of bytes, `b`, so individual bytes can be read by position."""),
        (52, 53, """A `guard` is an "unless" check: if any condition fails, the `else` branch runs, and it must leave the function. Here: the packet must be exactly 20 bytes, byte 0 (the version) must be 1, and bytes 2 and 3 must be valid activity and quality numbers. `let activity = Activity(rawValue: b[2])` tries to build an `Activity` from the number and gives `nil` for an unknown number, so a damaged packet is rejected rather than misread. The `else { return nil }` is that rejection."""),
        (54, 54, """A small helper that builds a 16-bit number from bytes `i` and `i+1`, **low byte first**: `UInt16(b[i]) | UInt16(b[i+1]) << 8`. In Swift the shift `<<` is done before the `|`, so this is the low byte combined with the high byte shifted left 8 places. It undoes what the firmware's `put32`-style code did."""),
        (55, 57, """The same for 32 bits: four bytes, shifts of 0, 8, 16 and 24."""),
        (58, 58, """Byte 1 holds the flag bits."""),
        (59, 64, """Builds the snapshot field by field. `u32(4)` reads the sequence from bytes 4 to 7, `u32(8)` the uptime, `u32(12)` the steps, `u16(16)` the cadence. `flags & 4 != 0 ? b[18] : nil` says: if bit 2 of the flags (value 4) is set, the heart rate is byte 18, otherwise `nil` (the `&` is applied before the `!=`). The oxygen works the same with bit 4 (value 16) and byte 19. The four true/false fields test bits 0, 1, 3 and 5 the same way. `quality` and `activity` were already decoded above."""),
        (65, 66, "End of `decode` and of the struct."),
    ],
)

BTM = dict(
    path="ios/HealthWatch/BluetoothManager.swift",
    lang="swift",
    title="BluetoothManager.swift: finding and talking to the watch",
    intro="""CoreBluetooth is Apple's Bluetooth framework. The phone is the **central**. It works by *delegation*: you create a manager object, tell it "send your news to me" (`delegate: self`), and then it calls methods on you when things happen: a device was found, a connection succeeded, a value changed. The delegate methods below are those calls, in the order they occur in practice.""",
    items=[
        (1, 3, """`CoreBluetooth` is the Bluetooth framework; `Combine` supplies the machinery behind `@Published`."""),
        (5, 6, """`@MainActor` means everything in this class runs on the main thread, which is where screens are drawn, so updating a published value cannot cause a thread conflict. The class is `final` (no subclasses), an `NSObject` (required for CoreBluetooth's delegate protocols) and an `ObservableObject` (screens can watch it and redraw when it changes)."""),
        (7, 11, """The five values the screens watch. `@Published` means "announce a change to anything watching". `private(set)` means other code may read but not change them. `devices` is the list of watches found by scanning; `snapshot` is the latest reading, `nil` until one arrives; `status` is the text shown on the Device screen; `connected` says whether a watch is connected; `invalidPackets` counts packets that failed to decode."""),
        (12, 12, """A reference to the history store (an optional, `nil` until the app wires them together). It is the only member that is not `private`, because `HealthWatchApp` sets it."""),
        (14, 15, """The CoreBluetooth manager and the watch currently chosen. The `!` after `CBCentralManager` means "starts empty but will be set in `init` before it is ever used"."""),
        (17, 20, """The initialiser. `super.init()` must come first for an `NSObject`. Then the manager is created with `delegate: self` (send events to this object) and `queue: .main` (deliver them on the main thread, which is what `@MainActor` assumes)."""),
        (21, 26, """Starts a scan. First check that Bluetooth is on (`poweredOn`); otherwise show a message and stop (`guard`). Then clear the device list, set the status, and scan only for devices advertising the HealthWatch service UUID, so other Bluetooth devices nearby are ignored. `CBUUID(nsuuid:)` converts the plain `UUID` to Core Bluetooth's type."""),
        (27, 32, """Connect to the watch the user tapped. Scanning stops first (it uses power), the watch is remembered, the status changes, and the connection is requested."""),
        (33, 35, """Disconnect. `if let peripheral { ... }` runs only if a watch is currently chosen; it unwraps the optional."""),
        (36, 36, "End of the class."),
        (38, 38, """An `extension` adds methods to an existing type. This one declares that the class handles both sets of CoreBluetooth events: those of the central manager (scanning and connecting) and those of a peripheral (reading values)."""),
        (39, 41, """Called when the phone's Bluetooth state changes (on, off, permission refused). Sets the status text accordingly."""),
        (42, 45, """Called each time the scan finds a device. If it is not already in the list (compared by `identifier`), add it. The scan reports the same device repeatedly, so without the check the list would fill with duplicates."""),
        (46, 51, """Called when the connection succeeds. Marks connected, then starts the next step: sets this object as the watch's delegate and asks it to discover its services, but only the HealthWatch one."""),
        (52, 55, """Called if the connection attempt fails. Shows the error text (`error?.localizedDescription`) or "Connection failed" if there is none; `??` supplies the fallback."""),
        (56, 60, """Called when the connection ends. Marks not connected and clears the snapshot so the screens stop showing old data."""),
        (61, 66, """Called when the watch has listed its services. If there was an error, show it and stop. Otherwise, for each service whose UUID matches ours, ask for its characteristics, again only the snapshot one. The `where` clause is a filter attached to the `for` loop."""),
        (67, 74, """Called when the watch lists the characteristics. For the snapshot one: `setNotifyValue(true, ...)` subscribes, so from now on the watch pushes every change; `readValue` also asks for the current value once, so something appears on screen immediately instead of after up to half a second; and the status becomes "Connected"."""),
        (75, 82, """Called **every time a new value arrives**, twice a second. `guard error == nil, let data = characteristic.value` requires no error and some data, otherwise count an invalid packet and stop. Then `WatchSnapshot.decode` turns the bytes into a snapshot; if that fails the counter goes up and the function stops. A good snapshot becomes the `snapshot` property (all the screens redraw) and is passed to the history store with `history?.add(value)` (the `?` means "only if the store is connected")."""),
        (83, 83, "End of the extension."),
    ],
)

